<h1>Video 095 — Thread Group (Multithreading Enhancement Part-1)</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java With OCJP/SCJP: Multithreading Enhancement  Part- 1|| Introduction || Thread Group</p>
<p>Source: real YouTube auto-captions (.transcripts/095-B2L-vVpFP7Y.txt). ASR decoded in place: thread group / thre group = ThreadGroup; subad / sub thread groups = sub thread groups; synchronizer block = synchronized block; demand = daemon; attach Leger / attach legar = Attach Listener; Finalizer = Finalizer (GC-related system thread); get a thread group = getThreadGroup(); get a Max priority / set a Max priority = getMaxPriority() / setMaxPriority(); interactive count / active count = activeCount(); inactive group count / active group count = activeGroupCount(); in numerate / enumerator = enumerate; set a demand = setDaemon; TG / teaching = sample group name &quot;TG&quot; / teaching; PG / CG = parent group / child group; keep subing / keep uping = keep writing.</p>
<p>Series intro to Multithreading Enhancements, then full ThreadGroup lecture: definition, Yahoo Messenger analogy, main/system hierarchy, constructors, important methods (especially setMaxPriority loophole), demos for activeCount/list, and program listing all active threads under system group (daemon flags).</p>
<h3>00:05 — Series: Multithreading enhancements (advanced)</h3>
<p>Next: enhancements related to multi-threading — a bit advanced; terminology may be new; take special care.</p>
<p>Agenda items announced for the enhancement series:</p>
<ul><li>ThreadGroup</li></ul>
<ul><li>ThreadLocal</li></ul>
<ul><li>Alternative to synchronized block</li></ul>
<ul><li>Concurrent package</li></ul>
<ul><li>Executor framework</li></ul>
<p>First concept: Thread Group.</p>
<h3>00:52 — What is a Thread Group?</h3>
<p>Thread group = a group of threads based on functionality, kept as a single unit.</p>
<p>Examples of grouping by function: all producer threads, all consumer threads, all printing threads.</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board:
 // Based on functionality we can group threads into a single unit
 // which is nothing but Thread Group
 // That is: Thread Group contains a group of threads</pre>
<h3>01:48 — Thread group can contain sub thread groups</h3>
<p>A thread group contains threads and can also contain sub thread groups (nested groups with their own threads Tx, Ty, …).</p>
<pre class='code'> // Thread group contains a group of threads
 // In addition to threads, thread group can also contain sub thread groups</pre>
<h3>02:42 — Advantage (Yahoo Messenger analogy)</h3>
<p>Advantage of maintaining threads as a group: perform common operations very easily.</p>
<p>Yahoo Messenger contacts:</p>
<ul><li>Friends group, Relatives group, …</li></ul>
<ul><li>Right-click group → send “Happy Friendship Day” to all friends in one shot.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Without groups: identify each friend and send individually.</li></ul>
<p>Same for threads:</p>
<ul><li>Suspend all consumer threads</li></ul>
<ul><li>Stop all producer threads</li></ul>
<ul><li>Set max priority for all printing threads</li></ul>
<ul><li>Set min priority for all header threads</li></ul>
<p>→ common operations become easy.</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board:
 // The main advantage of maintaining threads in the form of Thread Group is
 // we can perform common operations very easily</pre>
<h3>08:25 — Every thread belongs to some thread group</h3>
<p>Every thread in Java belongs to some thread group. Independently, without a thread group, there is no chance of a thread existing.</p>
<h3>08:51 — Demo: which group does main belong to?</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // How many threads? One — main
        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getThreadGroup().getName()
        );
        // prints main
    }
}</pre>
<p>Three “main” characters (do not confuse):</p>
<ol><li>main method</li></ol>
<ol><li>main thread</li></ol>
<ol><li>main thread group</li></ol>
<p>Main method is called by main thread; main thread belongs to main thread group. Default printed name: main.</p>
<h3>11:24 — Parent of main group = system group</h3>
<p>Every thread group is a child (directly or indirectly) of the system group — similar to how every class is a child of Object (Object is root for classes; system is root for thread groups).</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getThreadGroup().getName()
        );
        // prints main

        System.out.println(
            Thread.currentThread().getThreadGroup().getParent().getName()
        );
        // prints system
    }
}</pre>
<p>He compiles/runs: first line main, second system.</p>
<h3>15:50 — Board theory: hierarchy rules</h3>
<pre class='code'> // Every thread in Java belongs to some group
 // Main thread belongs to main group
 //
 // Every thread group in Java is the child group of system group
 // either directly or indirectly
 // Hence system group acts as root for all thread groups in Java</pre>
<h3>17:47 — What does system group contain?</h3>
<p>System group contains several system-level threads, for example:</p>
<ul><li>Finalizer (garbage collector related)</li></ul>
<ul><li>Reference Handler</li></ul>
<ul><li>Signal Dispatcher</li></ul>
<ul><li>Attach Listener</li></ul>
<ul><li>… and more (list can vary; don’t feel only these four)</li></ul>
<p>Common misconception: “every Java program has exactly two threads — main and GC.” Wrong — there are several system-level threads.</p>
<p>All system-level threads are daemon (he will show later).</p>
<h3>20:04 — Hierarchy diagram (spoken)</h3>
<p>system  (root)
  └── main  (child of system)
        └── (optional sub thread groups you create)
        └── main thread, and other threads you add under main</p>
<p>System group holds system threads (Finalizer, Attach Listener, Reference Handler, Signal Dispatcher, …).</p>
<p>Main group holds main thread (and any threads/groups created under it).</p>
<p>He asks students to take the diagram carefully (long silent board time ~21:40–23:05).</p>
<h3>23:22 — Program again next to diagram</h3>
<p>System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getThreadGroup().getName());
// prints main

System.out.println(
    Thread.currentThread().getThreadGroup().getParent().getName()
);
// prints system</p>
<h3>25:02 — ThreadGroup is a Java class</h3>
<p>ThreadGroup is a class in `java.lang`, direct child of `Object` (like Thread).</p>
<pre class='code'> // ThreadGroup is a Java class
 // present in java.lang package
 // and it is the direct child class of Object</pre>
<h3>26:33 — Constructor 1: `ThreadGroup(String name)`</h3>
<p>ThreadGroup g = new ThreadGroup(String groupName);
// Creates a new thread group with the specified group name
// The parent of this new group is the thread group
// of currently executing thread</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<p>ThreadGroup g1 = new ThreadGroup(&quot;First Group&quot;);
System.out.println(g1.getParent().getName());
// prints main
 // because this line executed by main → parent = main group</p>
<p>If the creating line were executed by a thread belonging to group XYZ, the new group would become child of XYZ.</p>
<h3>31:41 — Constructor 2: customize parent</h3>
<p>Requirement: new group should be child of some specified parent group (not only current thread’s group).</p>
<p>ThreadGroup g = new ThreadGroup(ThreadGroup pg, String groupName);
// Creates a new thread group with the specified group name
// The parent of this new thread group is the specified parent group</p>
<p>Example:</p>
<p>ThreadGroup g1 = new ThreadGroup(&quot;First Group&quot;);
ThreadGroup g2 = new ThreadGroup(g1, &quot;Second Group&quot;);
System.out.println(g1.getParent().getName());
// prints main
System.out.println(g2.getParent().getName());
// prints First Group</p>
<p>Hierarchy from this program:</p>
<p>system
 └── main
      └── First Group
           └── Second Group</p>
<h3>36:44 — Combined Test program (both constructors)</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ThreadGroup g1 = new ThreadGroup(&quot;First Group&quot;);
        System.out.println(g1.getParent().getName());
        // prints main

        ThreadGroup g2 = new ThreadGroup(g1, &quot;Second Group&quot;);
        System.out.println(g2.getParent().getName());
        // prints First Group
    }
}</pre>
<p>Diagram reminder: system → main → first group → second group.</p>
<h3>39:53 — Important methods of ThreadGroup</h3>
<p>Already used: getName(), getParent().</p>
<p>More important methods he lists and explains:</p>
<h3>40:39 — `String getName()`</h3>
<p>Returns name of the thread group.</p>
<pre class='code'>public String getName();
 // returns name of the thread group</pre>
<h3>41:55 — `int getMaxPriority()` / `void setMaxPriority(int p)`</h3>
<pre class='code'>public int getMaxPriority();
 // returns max priority of thread group

public void setMaxPriority(int p);
 // to set maximum priority of thread group</pre>
<p>Default max priority of a thread group is 10 (same idea as max thread priority 10).</p>
<h3>43:34 — Loophole / twist of `setMaxPriority` (board demo)</h3>
<p>ThreadGroup g1 = new ThreadGroup(&quot;TG&quot;); // or &quot;Teaching&quot;
 // default max priority of g1 = 10

Thread t1 = new Thread(g1, &quot;First Thread&quot;);
 // default thread priority = 5 → allowed (≤ 10)

Thread t2 = new Thread(g1, &quot;Second Thread&quot;);
 // priority 5 → allowed

g1.setMaxPriority(3);
 // group max becomes 3

Thread t3 = new Thread(g1, &quot;Third Thread&quot;);
 // newly added → priority becomes 3 (capped by group max)</p>
<p>Question: After lowering group max to 3, do existing t1/t2 priorities change from 5 to 3 automatically?</p>
<p>Answer: No. Threads already present with higher priority are not affected. New max applies to newly added threads.</p>
<p>System.out.println(t1.getPriority()); // prints 5
System.out.println(t2.getPriority()); // prints 5
System.out.println(t3.getPriority()); // prints 3</p>
<pre class='code'> // Board (dictated):
 // Threads in the thread group that already have higher priority
 // won&#x27;t be affected
 // But for newly added threads this max priority is applicable</pre>
<p>He runs ThreadGroupDemo / ThreadGroupDemo2 → output 5, 5, 3.</p>
<h3>54:41 — More methods</h3>
<p>`ThreadGroup getParent()`</p>
<pre class='code'>public ThreadGroup getParent();
 // returns parent group of current thread group</pre>
<p>`void list()`</p>
<pre class='code'>public void list();
 // prints information about thread group to the console
 // (threads + sub thread groups)</pre>
<p>`int activeCount()`</p>
<pre class='code'>public int activeCount();
 // returns number of active threads present in the thread group</pre>
<p>`int activeGroupCount()`</p>
<pre class='code'>public int activeGroupCount();
 // returns number of active groups (sub groups)
 // present in the current thread group</pre>
<h3>58:17 — `enumerate(Thread[] t)`</h3>
<p>Copy all active threads of this thread group into the provided thread array.</p>
<p>Thread[] t = new Thread[10];
g.enumerate(t);
 // all active threads in g copied into t
 // Sub thread group threads also will be considered</p>
<p>Use case: system group — copy all active threads, then print names.</p>
<pre class='code'> // To copy all active threads of this thread group
 // into provided thread array
 // Sub thread group threads also will be considered</pre>
<h3>1:01:42 — `enumerate(ThreadGroup[] g)`</h3>
<pre class='code'>public int enumerate(ThreadGroup[] g);
 // to copy all active sub thread groups
 // into thread group array</pre>
<h3>1:02:24 — Daemon / interrupt / destroy at group level</h3>
<pre class='code'>public boolean isDaemon();
 // check whether the thread group is daemon or not

public void setDaemon(boolean b);
 // change daemon nature of thread group

public void interrupt();
 // interrupt all waiting or sleeping threads
 // present in the thread group

public void destroy();
 // destroy thread group and its sub thread groups</pre>
<h3>1:04:33 — Demo 3: `activeCount`, `activeGroupCount`, `list`</h3>
<pre class='code'>class MyThread extends Thread {
    MyThread(ThreadGroup g, String name) {
        super(g, name); // associate thread with group + name
    }

    public void run() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Child Thread&quot;);
        try {
            Thread.sleep(5000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        }
    }
}

class ThreadGroupDemo3 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ThreadGroup pg = new ThreadGroup(&quot;Parent Group&quot;);
        ThreadGroup cg = new ThreadGroup(pg, &quot;Child Group&quot;);

        MyThread t1 = new MyThread(pg, &quot;Child Thread 1&quot;);
        MyThread t2 = new MyThread(pg, &quot;Child Thread 2&quot;);
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        // both print &quot;Child Thread&quot; then sleep 5s (still active)

        System.out.println(pg.activeCount());
        // prints 2

        System.out.println(pg.activeGroupCount());
        // prints 1  (child group)

        pg.list();
        // prints group info: parent group maxpri=10,
        // Child Thread 1 / 2 priority 5 under parent,
        // plus java.lang.ThreadGroup[name=Child Group,maxpri=10]

        Thread.sleep(10000);
        // within 10s both children finish (slept only 5s) → dead

        System.out.println(pg.activeCount());
        // prints 0

        System.out.println(pg.activeGroupCount());
        // prints 1  (child group still there)

        pg.list();
        // list shows child group remains; active threads gone
    }
}</pre>
<p>Observed run (as narrated):</p>
<p>2
Child Thread
Child Thread
1
java.lang.ThreadGroup[name=Parent Group,maxpri=10]
    Thread[Child Thread 1,5,Parent Group]
    Thread[Child Thread 2,5,Parent Group]
    java.lang.ThreadGroup[name=Child Group,maxpri=10]
... (after 10 seconds) ...
0
1
java.lang.ThreadGroup[name=Parent Group,maxpri=10]
    java.lang.ThreadGroup[name=Child Group,maxpri=10]</p>
<h3>1:16:10 — Hierarchy diagram for Demo 3</h3>
<p>system
 └── main
      └── Parent Group
           ├── Child Thread 1
           ├── Child Thread 2
           └── Child Group   (sub group)</p>
<h3>1:17:55 — Who associates thread to a group?</h3>
<p>Constructor job: associate thread with a group.</p>
<p>super(g, name) calls Thread constructor (ThreadGroup, String) — that associates name and group.</p>
<h3>1:18:45 — Program: display all active thread names under system group</h3>
<p>Requirement: write a program to display all active thread names belonging to system group and its child groups.</p>
<p>Approach: get system group reference → enumerate into array → print name + isDaemon().</p>
<pre class='code'>class ThreadGroupDemo4 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ThreadGroup system =
            Thread.currentThread()
                .getThreadGroup()
                .getParent(); // main&#x27;s parent = system

        Thread[] t = new Thread[system.activeCount()];
        system.enumerate(t);

        for (Thread t1 : t) {
            System.out.println(
                t1.getName() + &quot; ........ &quot; + t1.isDaemon()
            );
        }
    }
}</pre>
<p>Sample / expected style of output (exact set can vary by JVM):</p>
<p>Reference Handler ........ true
Finalizer ........ true
Signal Dispatcher ........ true
Attach Listener ........ true
main ........ false</p>
<p>He runs ThreadGroupDemo4.java and sees roughly: Reference Handler true, Finalizer true, Signal Dispatcher true, Attach Listener true, main false — matches expectation. System-level threads are daemon; main is non-daemon.</p>
<h3>1:28:28 — Close of Part-1 enhancements</h3>
<p>Summary covered: what is thread group, constructors, methods, how to create groups, how many active threads in system / system group contents. End of Multithreading Enhancement Part-1 (Thread Group). Next topics continue in later enhancement parts.</p>
